  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1254 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nToday - Sunday: Dry Today, Storms for Sunday\n\nAfter a trough axis moved past the area yesterday evening, the flow \npattern across the region shifts to northwest flow for today. With \nthe northwest wind shift, there is a low potential for smoke to come \nback, however this should mostly stay aloft and not impact the near \nsurface area. Focusing on the precipitation chances, the boundary \nthat as helping storms initiate for yesterday has shifted south. \nThis will keep any storm chances at bay and result in a pretty nice \nday with highs in the mid 70s to low 80s. Dewpoints decrease \ncompared to the past couple of days into the mid 50s to low 60s. By \nlater this evening, a low forms just of the central Rockies. This \nhelps to push the boundary back northward. In doing so, \nsouthwesterly flow returns and dewpoints increase back into the mid \n60s. Incoming PWATs are in the 1.5 to 2.0\" range so storms that do \nform would be efficient rain producers in short order. Now there is \ngood model agreement on a decent wave moving through the Upper \nMidwest Sunday morning, however the convective evolution is \nuncertain. Given that we are in northwest flow aloft, this could \nfavor a MCS moving through the area. Most CAMs do show some sort of \nstorm complex impacting portions of the forecast area Sunday morning \nwith differences in exact location and intensity. \n\nHeading into the afternoon and evening on Sunday, the low that \nforced the boundary further north for the morning, shifts further \neast and creates another push of southwesterly flow, keeping PWATs \nin the 1.5 to 2.0\" range and dewpoints in the upper 60s to mid 70s. \nWhile some of the CAMs are beginning to show signs of convection by \nthe later evening, uncertainty resides in how convection will go. If \nwe can clear out fast enough, then the atmosphere could recover, \nwhich some models are showing that owing to precipitation ending by \nearly afternoon and convection not forming until the later evening \nhours. Other models have most of the area under a strong CAP into \nthe evening. Needless to say that even a day out the storm evolution \nis quite uncertain for Sunday morning and evening. \n\nMonday - Tuesday: Continued Storm Potential\n\nThe same boundary that will impact precipitation chances this \nweekend is expected to sit somewhere between central Iowa and \nsouthern Minnesota. This will provide a few opportunities for storms \nto impact this area. Looking at LREF clustering, all of them have \nanother decent wave pegged to move through the Upper Midwest Tuesday \nmorning, however similar to this weekend, details regarding early \nnext week's storm potential is quite uncertain, but will be refined \nin the coming days.\n\nWednesday - Saturday: Cooler and Drier\n\nEnsembles are in good agreement that a cooler airmass shifts \nsouthward into the Upper Midwest for the latter portions of next \nweek. This will bring temperatures down into the low to mid 70s. The \nbetter rain chances remain to our south as the front looks to stay \nfurther south, however with this front not too far away, rain chances \ncould increase if this front were to move back north. Currently, \nthere are some GEFS and EPS members showing increased rain chances \nfor this period, however the majority of them are dry. The better \nrain chance is looking like Friday into Saturday.",
